---
import { teamRules, submissionRequirements, scoringFramework, finalistRubric } from '../../data/hackathons';
import FormCta from './FormCta.astro';
import { href } from '../../data/project';
---
<FormCta kind="participant" label="Register for Hackathon" id="rules-registration-note"/><section class="section" id="teams"><h2>Individual entry and teams</h2><ul>{teamRules.map(rule=><li>{rule}</li>)}</ul><p><a class="text-link" href={`${href('hackathons')}#qualification`}>Mandatory qualification and selection →</a></p></section>
<section class="section" id="submission"><h2>Final project submission</h2><p>Every project must be a working MVP, executable prototype or functional proof of concept. A slide deck or documented walkthrough alone is insufficient. Any programming language or framework; standalone implementations are acceptable after mandatory AeglysAI qualification.</p><ul>{submissionRequirements.map(rule=><li>{rule}</li>)}</ul><p>Narrated screen recordings or silent captioned demonstrations are accepted. Webcam footage is not mandatory. A deployed demo URL is optional. Submit only sanitized evidence; no private participant, credential or patent-related data.</p><a class="text-link" href={href('community/submit')}>Prepare the final submission →</a></section>
<section class="section" id="judging"><h2>Two-round judging</h2><h3>Round 1 — technical screening</h3><p>Review source code, working prototype, required video and reproducibility. Round 1 determines finalist selection.</p><ul>{scoringFramework.map(row=><li>{row.category} — {row.weight} points</li>)}</ul><h3>Round 2 — finalist review</h3><p>Shortlisted finalists give a five-minute live demonstration over Google Meet followed by five minutes of technical Q&amp;A. Round 2 determines final ranking; do not combine round scores.</p><ul>{finalistRubric.map(row=><li>{row.category} — {row.weight} points</li>)}</ul><p>Proposed capacity: five to ten finalist teams, subject to submission quality and organizer capacity. This is not a guaranteed finalist count. Each rubric totals 100 points; equally weighted, non-conflicted judges score independently and their scores are averaged within each round.</p><p>Round 1 ties: functionality, intelligent feature, then reproducibility. Round 2 ties: live demonstration, technical understanding, then solution quality and safety. Remaining ties receive equal additional demonstration/Q&amp;A and independent panel review; unresolved ties receive a shared rank with any capacity decision explained under published rules.</p><p>Reviewers disclose conflicts privately and recuse; organizers assign unconflicted replacements. Technical clarification uses equivalent time and scope, is logged privately and cannot permit undisclosed post-deadline feature development. Scores, roster mappings and private comments remain in private Google Sheets/Drive. Judges, dates and results are not confirmed.</p></section>
